Response to the team’s refinement

Choice: A. Confirm Remediation Scope and Mechanism Alignment Review as the first decision product, with two changes.

Narrow the decision. The decision is not “alignment”. It is which closed material issues receive an independent closure-quality review this quarter. That is a real selection problem with finite review capacity.
Reverse the experiment order. Your Gate 1 (pattern clustering against the taxonomy) is not a prerequisite for this product. Running it first delays the first decision for no gain.

Why A beats the alternatives:

Control relevance and rating challenge depend on the weakest links in the data.
A severe-and-novel product has too few cases to validate.
Pattern discovery with no product risks the “interesting but unused” outcome you already flagged.
Remediation alignment can be checked by an expert reading two documents. It needs no denominators for its core classes and sits directly on a decision someone already owns.

Why reverse the order: classes A to D compare what the issue said was wrong with what the action and closure note say was done. That needs no clustering, no incidents and no exposure data. Class E (similar events after closure) needs targeted retrieval: given the issue’s mechanism and scope, find later incidents that plausibly share it. That is a retrieval task, not a catalogue. Mechanism-labelling feasibility is still tested, inside the alignment labelling. The pattern catalogue stays the strategic foundation, and the expert labels from this work become its seed data.

Where AI will and will not add value: some of C (implementation unclear) and D (no verification) can be caught by simple rules, such as an empty or very short closure note. Language understanding is needed mainly for A, B and E. The experiment must test this, because if rules capture most of the value, that is a finding in its own right and a cheaper product.

