1. Experiment and product structure
A. Experiment: can we reproduce expert judgement of issue-to-action alignment, and retrieve post-closure similar incidents better than current methods?
B. Usable product: a ranked shortlist for one named reviewer in one existing forum.
C. Postponed: pattern clustering, controls, rating challenges, products, tasks, structural hypotheses, prediction.

Two gates are preferable to the full MVP. The full MVP would leave a failure hard to attribute to pattern validity, link quality, narrative quality or the model.

2. What “beat” means

Thresholds are starting proposals to agree with model risk before any results are seen. They are judgements, not derived values.

Baselines

B1: structural rules (missing or short closure text, single action, standard wording).
B2: existing taxonomy match (root cause hierarchy).
B3: keyword and BM25 search on descriptions.
B4: existing human cross-references.
B5: a subject-matter expert (SME) with current tools and a fixed time box. This is the real test of “would not readily identify”.

Metrics and thresholds

Alignment classes: model agreement with adjudicated expert labels at least 80% of the experts’ own agreement, and macro-F1 at least 0.15 above B1.
Retrieval: at k=20, at least 25% more expert-confirmed relevant incidents than the best baseline at equal or better precision. Relevance is judged blind on pooled results from all methods, so the new method is not favoured.
Stability: the above holds on a locked test set made up of the most recent closures.

Minimum expert agreement: kappa or alpha of at least 0.6.

Stop:

Agreement is below 0.4 after one redefinition of the labelling guidance.
The model does not beat the best baseline.
Rules match the model on every class.

Proceed despite modest improvement: only if the cases unique to the new method are expert-confirmed as material (cross-label or cross-business hits the baselines cannot reach), or review time falls by at least half at equal quality.

3. Decomposing the pattern object

Yes, decompose. A composite object clusters on noise from context and consequence. Use six elements:

Mechanism: a short structured statement (what failed, at which step, in which direction). This is the thing embedded and compared.
Occurrence: the incident or issue record.
Operating context: process, application, product and scope, attached separately.
Enabling condition: change, hand-off or third party, attached separately.
Consequence: used for materiality only.
Intervention: the action or response.

A “failure pattern” becomes a derived view: a mechanism within a defined context slice. It is not a stored object. Recurrence then means recurrence of a mechanism in a stated context. Records may carry several mechanism tags. This is more stable because mechanism text embeds without context noise. The cost is more labelling objects, but each label is shorter.

The MVP uses mechanism, occurrence, context (process and application only) and intervention. Consequence is used only to rank.

4. Role of issues
Issues do not join incident clusters. Each gets its own weakness statement, built mainly from the control-gap detail and description.
An issue without incidents can be assessed for alignment but cannot form a failure mechanism.
IS_THR_CTRL_GAP is a stratifier and a filter. Test its precision on the labelled sample before relying on it.
Issue-to-action links are the strongest cross-reference, because actions are raised from issues, but verify them on the sample. Issue-to-control links are weak evidence and out of the MVP.
Umbrella and programme issues are detected from signals such as many linked actions, broad text, several mechanisms and programme wording. They are excluded from the first release and reported separately.
The stated root cause is recorded as a claim with its source. It is never used as truth.
Yes, weakness statements are a separate vocabulary. The link between a weakness and a failure mechanism is a model-derived hypothesis (“this weakness would permit this mechanism”).
5. Remediation alignment

Compare the issue description, stated root cause and control-gap detail against the action description and closure summary, then against incidents before and after closure within the same process and application scope. Dates: action completion, issue closure, incident occurrence.

Use your seven classes as tags with one primary tag per case:

Tier 1 (document-internal): A, B, C, D and G.
Tier 2 (needs incidents): E.
Deferred: F, because proving movement of a problem into another scope is hard.

Data readiness risk: if most closure notes lack verification wording, D will fire on nearly everything and stop discriminating. Measure that base rate first. If it is very high, report D as a population finding for the issue-management policy owner and rank individual cases on A, B and E.

6. Controls

Fully deferred. Control display in the case card is context only. When the track opens, it runs as six separate gates:

Relevance retrieval.
Role classification (prevent, detect, mitigate, recover).
Scope alignment.
Temporal alignment.
Assessment alignment.
Rating-challenge candidates.

Gates 4 and 5 are mostly deterministic date and field rules, not LLM judgements. Only 1 to 3 need a model.

7. Approach B, single domain first

Approach A produces many unreviewable clusters, widens privacy and governance scope, and calibrates against taxonomy drift across domains that behave differently. Build and validate in one domain, then test transfer to a second. The transfer test is itself informative.

Ideal pilot domain

Characteristic	Target
Closed material issues with actions	Enough to label several hundred cases
Incident volume	Thousands, so post-closure retrieval is meaningful but reviewable
Narrative quality	Closure notes with real content in most records
Issue-action link coverage	Present for most closed material issues
Impact	Meaningful financial or customer consequence
Process and application data	Available for scope labels
SME availability	Two independent reviewers plus an adjudicator
Existing taxonomy	Moderate quality, neither trivially good nor absent
Sponsor	A named reviewer who owns closure challenge

Payments is plausible because process and linkage work exists and exposure data such as transaction volumes is likely. Existing work also means the domain may be unrepresentative. Profile three candidate domains against this table for a week, then choose.

8. Exposure normalisation
Tier 1: no exposure data needed.
Tier 2 essential: a period-matched measure of overall incident activity in the domain, and reporting indicators (below).
Desirable: transaction or customer volumes for the scope, change and release counts, application usage, provider volumes.

Can be reported without exposure data:

Counts of retrieved similar events in equal-length windows before and after closure.
Dates, severity and loss.

Must not be claimed:

Rates, improvement or deterioration, comparative effectiveness, “reduced risk”.

Process and application footprint (number of steps, systems touched) measures complexity, not exposure. Do not use it as a denominator.

Reporting indicators, at process level only: median lag from occurrence to logging, share of incidents logged as No Impact or Low, and logging volume trend.

Raw counts are still decision-useful when similar events appear after closure in the same scope with none in an equal prior window, or for any single Severe event. Tier 1 uses no counts.

9. Evidence layers

I would add a fifth executive state: Inferred, not yet reviewed. Without it, unreviewed model output blends into confirmed findings. “Inferred and expert-confirmed” should require agreement from two reviewers, or one plus adjudication.

Analytical	Executive
Observed [O]	Directly recorded
Model-derived [M], reviewed	Inferred and expert-confirmed
Model-derived [M], unreviewed	Inferred, not yet reviewed
Statistical association [S]	Statistically associated
Expert judgement [E]	Expert assessment
Causal [C]	Causal conclusion not established (always shown)
10. Gates

Thresholds are proposals to be agreed up front. Method repair is permitted at G1 to G3 and G4 only within the stated limits. The test set is touched once.

Gate	Metric and threshold	Approver	If missed
G1 Data readiness	Usable narrative in at least 80% of in-scope issues and actions, valid closure dates in at least 90%, issue-action link in at least 80%	Sponsor and data owner	One retry with another domain or extraction fix, then stop
G2 Labelling feasibility	Kappa or alpha of at least 0.6 on alignment class and mechanism match. 0.4 to 0.6 allows one redefinition.	Independent SME lead and model risk	Stop below 0.4 after redefinition
G3 Performance	As in section 2, on the locked test set	Model risk	Two tuning cycles on development data, then stop
G4 Usefulness	At least 70% of confirmed cases lead the reviewer to name a distinct review action. Classes with no distinct action are merged.	Primary user	Redefine classes once
G5 Decision yield	At least 50% of the top 30 judged “worth review” by the reviewer, and at least 5 confirmed cases that B5 did not surface	Primary user and sponsor	Stop the product, keep the analytics
G6 Adoption	Named reviewer commits to use the queue in an existing forum for two consecutive cycles and logs decisions	Primary user’s manager	Stop. No repair, since the cause is organisational.
Final use-case specification

1. Use case. For closed, material issues in one pilot domain, compare what the issue said was wrong with what the actions and closure note say was done, and with similar incidents after closure, and produce an evidence-graded shortlist for independent review. It does not conclude that remediation failed.

2. Primary user. The Line 2 lead for remediation and issue-closure oversight in the pilot domain. They hold independent challenge authority and do not own the actions being reviewed.

3. Primary decision. Which closed material issues receive an independent closure-quality review this quarter.

4. Primary analytical object. The remediation alignment case: an issue, its closed action set, closure evidence, scope, and a post-closure incident retrieval set. Mechanism statements are supporting objects.

5. Source tables.

Issues
Actions
Issue-action cross-reference
Incidents (Tier 2 only)
Incident-to-process and incident-to-application references
Process and application tables for scope names only

Excluded: controls, assessments, obligations, products, tasks.

6. Required fields.

Issues: identifier, description, root cause and hierarchy, rating, type, IS_THR_CTRL_GAP and detail, closure summary, open and close dates, scope references.
Actions: identifier, description, closure summary, completion date, linked issue.
Incidents: description, cause and root cause hierarchy, occurrence, detection and logging dates, impact ratings, financial impact, scope references.
Owner fields: dropped at ingestion.

7. First experiment.

1a, alignment: two SMEs label alignment classes from issue and action text, blind to the model. Compare the model and the rules baseline with the adjudicated labels. Then rank the remaining cases and have SMEs review the top of the list plus a random sample of unflagged cases to find misses.
1b, retrieval: only if 1a passes. For each sampled issue, retrieve post-closure incidents with each method and judge the pooled results blind.
Why this can invalidate the concept: 1a alone fails it if experts cannot agree or if rules do as well as the model.

8. Validation sample. Size comes from the precision needed, not from a round number. As a planning assumption, use about 400 double-labelled issue-action cases and about 80 retrieval pools, revised after the first 100 labels reveal agreement and class prevalence.

Stratify by issue rating, closure year (to cover taxonomy changes), sub-process and closure note length.
Oversample rare classes so each of A to F has about 30 examples.
Keep a separate simple random sample of about 150 cases for prevalence estimates, because oversampling distorts prevalence.
Split into development, calibration and a locked test set of the most recent closures.
Two independent labellers, a third to adjudicate, and record labelling time.

9. Baselines. B1 to B5 from section 2. Alignment is compared with B1 and B5. Retrieval is compared with B2 to B5.

10. Success measures.

Analytical: inter-expert agreement, model agreement against adjudicated labels, gain over B1 and B5, retrieval gain, stability on the locked set.
Decision usefulness: precision of the top 30, confirmed cases baselines missed, reviewer time per case, distinct review actions named, and the share of completed reviews that find a real gap.

11. Stop conditions.

G1 fails twice.
Agreement is below 0.4 after redefinition.
The model does not beat the best baseline and has no material unique hits.
Fewer than 5 confirmed cases beyond what B5 finds.
No named user commits to a forum.

Materially redesign if rules match the model (ship the rules) or if all value sits in one class (narrow to it).

12. MVP output (illustrative, invented figures).

Case card: ISS-0000, rated High, closed [date]

Executive layer

Directly recorded: the action was a procedure update and refresher training. The closure note contains no verification statement.
Inferred and expert-confirmed (2 of 2 reviewers): the issue describes a system detection weakness, and the action addresses staff behaviour. The issue references three channels, and the action names one.
Inferred, not yet reviewed: four later incidents in two channels may share the mechanism.
Expert assessment: independent review recommended.
Causal conclusion not established: we cannot say whether the action worked.

Analytical layer

Tags: A (mechanism mismatch), B (scope mismatch), D (no verification wording), E (post-closure similar events). Primary tag: A.
Counts: 4 retrieved incidents in the 18 months after closure and 6 in the prior 18. No exposure adjustment, no rate computed.
Confidence: moderate.
Unknowns: a system change outside the action record, volume changes, reporting changes.

13. Claim boundaries.

Can conclude: what the documents say, whether the action text addresses the stated weakness and scope, whether verification is evidenced, and whether similar events were observed after closure, with counts.
Cannot conclude: that remediation failed or worked, an incidence rate or trend, that a control is ineffective, that semantic similarity proves a relationship, or anything about people or teams.

14. Deferred scope.

Pattern clustering and the enterprise catalogue.
Controls, assessments and rating challenges.
Class F (expansion into other scopes).
Products, tasks, obligations and structural hypotheses.
Prediction and any causal or twin modelling.
Owner, team or business-unit comparisons.
Umbrella and programme issues.

15. Expansion path. If the MVP passes G6, the next capability is a validated mechanism catalogue for the pilot domain, built from the expert mechanism labels already collected. It turns Tier 2 from per-issue retrieval into mechanism-level recurrence, and the same labels seed the later control-relevance track.

Main risk to watch: raising closure-quality challenge could be read as an audit of Line 1 and discourage issue raising or closure. The owner-field removal, the role-level user and the no-blame wording are the safeguards, and the reporting indicators in section 8 should be monitored from the first cycle.
